"executive_summary": "This paper analyzes the Cauchy (initial value) problem for a general class of two-dimensional nonlinear nonlocal wave equations that model anti-plane shear motions in nonlocal elastic materials. The nonlocal character of the model is encoded through a convolution integral in the spatial variables, whose Fourier transform is nonnegative and satisfies a growth condition at infinity. The authors establish well-posedness, conditions for global existence, and conditions for finite-time blow-up of solutions for initial data in L2 Sobolev spaces. The central object is the equation w_tt = (β * ∂F/∂w_x)_x + (β * ∂F/∂w_y)_y, where F is a nonlinear function of |∇w|² and β is the convolution kernel. The parameter r in the kernel's Fourier bound controls the regularizing effect of the nonlocal behavior: as r increases, the regularizing effect increases. The paper extends one-dimensional nonlinear nonlocal Boussinesq-type techniques to two dimensions. Local existence is proved for s > 2, and improved to s ≥ 1 when r > 3. Global existence is established under a positivity condition on F together with a growth condition r > 4 and an energy condition. Blow-up is shown to occur in the L∞-norm of |∇w| when the initial energy is negative and a certain inequality on F' holds. The authors note that when β is taken as the Dirac measure, the model reduces to the quasilinear wave equation of conventional elasticity. The work is purely theoretical and contains no numerical experiments, datasets, or computational simulations. The main tools are the Fourier transform, Sobolev embedding, energy conservation, and Gronwall's lemma. The paper is situated within the theory of nonlocal elasticity developed by Kröner, Eringen, Kunin, and others, and it extends prior one-dimensional studies. The results are presented as theorems and lemmas with rigorous proofs, and the paper concludes with an acknowledgement of support from the Scientific and Technological Research Council of Turkey (TUBITAK).",
